NAME
       systemd.unit - the format of systemd unit files

SYNOPSIS
       NAME.service, NAME.target, NAME.timer, NAME.socket, NAME.mount,
       NAME.path ...

DESCRIPTION
       A unit file describes something systemd manages. Its name ends with
       the unit type. The file is in INI style: sections in square brackets,
       then KEY=VALUE lines. # and ; start comments. Every unit type uses the
       [Unit] and [Install] sections described here; each type adds its own
       section ([Service], [Timer], [Mount] ...).

   Where unit files live
       /usr/lib/systemd/system/
              Units installed by packages. Do not edit these: an update
              would overwrite your change.

       /etc/systemd/system/
              Your own units, and changes to packaged ones. A file here with
              the same name as one in /usr/lib/systemd/system replaces it
              completely.

       /etc/systemd/system/NAME.d/*.conf
              Drop-in files: only the settings they contain are added to or
              change the original unit. systemctl edit NAME makes one
              (override.conf). To clear a list setting before giving new
              values, assign it empty first (ExecStart= then ExecStart=...).

       ~/.config/systemd/user/
              A user's own units, for systemctl --user.

       After creating or changing a file, run systemctl daemon-reload.
       systemctl cat NAME shows the unit with all its drop-ins.

THE [Unit] SECTION
       Description=TEXT
              A short name shown by systemctl status and in the journal.

       Documentation=URLS
              Where to read more (man:, https:).

       Wants=UNITS
              Start these units too; it does not matter if they fail.

       Requires=UNITS
              Start these too; if they fail or stop, stop this one.

       After=UNITS, Before=UNITS
              Order only: start this unit after (or before) those, when both
              are being started. Wants= and After= are usually given
              together (After=network-online.target with
              Wants=network-online.target).

       Conflicts=UNITS
              Starting this stops those, and the other way round.

       ConditionPathExists=PATH
              Start only if PATH exists (skipped quietly otherwise).

THE [Install] SECTION
       Used only by systemctl enable and disable.

       WantedBy=TARGETS
              systemctl enable makes a link in
              /etc/systemd/system/TARGET.wants/, so the unit starts whenever
              that target starts. Services that should run at boot use
              WantedBy=multi-user.target; user services use
              WantedBy=default.target; timers use WantedBy=timers.target.

       RequiredBy=TARGETS
              The same, as a Requires= dependency.

       Alias=NAMES
              Other names for the unit, linked at enable time.

TARGETS
       A target groups units into a state of the system, like the old run
       levels: multi-user.target (text console with network),
       graphical.target (with the graphical login), rescue.target (single
       user, file systems mounted), emergency.target (only a root shell, root
       file system read-only). systemctl get-default and set-default show and
       set the one used at boot.

EXAMPLES
       A drop-in that makes a service restart when it fails:

           mkdir -p /etc/systemd/system/httpd.service.d
           printf '[Service]\nRestart=on-failure\n' \
               > /etc/systemd/system/httpd.service.d/restart.conf
           systemctl daemon-reload
           systemctl restart httpd

       A small unit and how to turn it on:

           [Unit]
           Description=Lab backup
           After=network-online.target
           Wants=network-online.target

           [Service]
           Type=oneshot
           ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/backup.sh

           [Install]
           WantedBy=multi-user.target

           systemctl daemon-reload
           systemctl enable --now backup.service

